course: dictionary
lesson: 5966
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Matter is a good example: it can be a thing, a problem, or a verb meaning "to be important". To find the right meaning, read the words around it, then try your meaning in the sentence.
explain: «Your feelings matter.» Here matter is a verb, and it means your feelings are important. «What's the matter?» asks what is wrong, and «a serious matter» is a subject or problem to deal with.
explain: In science, matter means what everything is made of. In a maths problem, «the order does not matter» means you can swap the numbers and get the same answer. Look at the subject of the sentence to see which meaning you need.
fact: Matter (noun) is what everything is made of; all things that take up space and have mass are matter.
fact: A matter (noun) is a subject or problem to deal with, as in «a serious matter».
fact: To matter (verb) means to be important, as in «your feelings matter».
fact: «What's the matter?» asks what is wrong.
fact: «It doesn't matter» means it is not important, and «no matter what» means whatever happens.
exercise: «The order does not matter» in a maths problem means | The answer will always be wrong | The numbers are too big to use | *You can swap the numbers and the answer stays the same | You must put the numbers in order from smallest to biggest
exercise: «What's the matter?» asks | Where is the bus? | *What is wrong? | Who won the race? | Why is it raining?
exercise: In science, matter is | Something that is important to you | A problem to deal with | *Anything that takes up space and has mass | A rule about maths
test: «Spots come mainly from hormones, skin and genes; one chocolate bar will not cause a breakout, though very sugary diets may matter for some people.» Here matter means | What everything is made of | A problem to deal with | *To be important, or to make a difference | To stop working
test: «Think of your class newspaper with ten stories and room for four: whoever chooses is shaping what everyone thinks matters.» Here matters means | *Is important | Is made of tiny particles | Is a problem to solve | Is wrong
exam: In «Solids, liquids and gases are all forms of matter», what does matter mean? | To be important | *Anything that takes up space and has mass | A serious problem to deal with | Something that is wrong
